          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I smoke and I have been diagnosed with COPD.",
          "answer": "Stopping smoking is the most important step you can take with COPD. The Global respiratory strategy report says smoking cessation \"is the only intervention shown to alter the rate of decline in lung function\" (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*). Quitting does not reverse the damage already done, but it can slow further decline.\n\nOther things the same guidance covers:\n\n- **Pulmonary rehabilitation**, a structured programme of supervised exercise and education, improves breathlessness and exercise ability more than inhalers do, according to the same report.\n- **Vaccinations** against influenza, pneumococcal disease, COVID-19 and, where recommended, RSV can reduce flare-ups.\n- **Regular reviews** should happen at least once a year, and after any flare-up or change in inhaled treatment.\n\nA cardiology guideline, *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention*, notes that combined behavioural and medication support for quitting works better than advice alone. Talk to your clinician or pharmacist about which quit-smoking options suit you. I can't recommend any specific product or dose.\n\nIf you get severe difficulty breathing, such as being unable to speak in full sentences or feeling you cannot get enough air, seek emergency care straight away (*Severe difficulty breathing*, emergency guidance).\n\nThis is general educational information, not medical advice. Your clinician can assess your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
