          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does stopping actually change anything at this stage?",
          "answer": "Yes, the guidance I found says stopping still matters after a COPD diagnosis. The Global respiratory strategy report (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*) says smoking cessation \"is the only intervention shown to alter the rate of decline in lung function.\" That means quitting is about slowing future loss of lung function. It does not undo damage that has already happened.\n\nThe same report says that when a smoker is diagnosed, quitting is the first step, and that other measures such as rehabilitation and vaccination sit alongside it rather than replacing it.\n\nThe search did not return anything on how much benefit someone at a later stage of COPD would get from quitting, or on what the benefit looks like over time. Your clinician can talk through what quitting would mean for your own lung function and breathing.\n\nIf you have severe difficulty breathing, such as being unable to speak in full sentences, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
